Date: Thu, 4 Nov 1993 06:39:24 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Future Science

Nick Szabo reacts to my comments:

> > > With fitness cases of 1 million instructions
>
> > This, however is certainly unreasonable....Imagine a simulation of
> > something with the structural complexity of a bacterium (surely
> > a lower bound)
> 
> Simulating a bacterium is a lower bound?

Well, maybe I don't understand the point you were making in the following
paragraph, where I saw the 1e6 figure:

* For GP-style sexual crossover we have (very roughty) 
* 1 billion years*1 generation/yr*1e12 organisms = 1e21 fitness cases 
* for evolution of life.  With fitness cases of 1 million 
* instructions and 1% of civilization's projected computing power in 2013 ...

Even applied to testing the theories that would evolve in a GP
physicist, I find the 1e6 figure off by many orders of magnitude.
I can't see that GP physicists who consist of simple symbolic
regression with common mathematical operators acting on some small
dataset are going to discover much of anything new.  Even modest
data size will chew up gigaops of falsification for any remotely
plausible theory (and note that the average fitness of a GP
generation [after a little while] is pretty high, so don't count
on a vast majority of very bad [easily falsified] theories).

Further, very often new science has to develop new mathematics,
the operator set will have to evolve as well.  That is another source
of computational difficulty.

As you know from past interaction, I'm a great GP fan, so I'm not
arguing that your GP scientists are impossible, just much more
difficult than you imply.

> With GP and good data, it well might take a big chunk out of those
> 1,000 years.  In its sole foray into science
> so far,  GP recapitulated Kepler's laws, much more advanced than 
> anything the Egyptians came up with in astronomy, in less than a day.

This is interesting.  My first problem is "good data".  That can be
required if one knows _exactly_ what phenomenon needs explanation
and can massage the anomalous measurements into a small form.
Does science really progress this way? (maybe it does, I really don't
know).  If so, I'd love to get the preened datasets that today's
physicists are puzzling over!

> And GP is far from the only tool available.  The math known to the 
> Egyptians is child's play for Mathematica.  Heck, most of the math 
> known before the 20th century is child's play for Mathematica.

Well, no kidding!  I think it would be vastly unfair, for example, to
take some GP with a full suite of analysis tools, "rediscover" the
laws of gravity, and claim success.  That's simply hindsight.  I
remember studying another automated regression artifact, called Bacon,
that massaged data and "discovered" PV = nRT from a table of measurements.
Big deal.  A diligent child could have done the same.  Yet somehow
I feel that the science of the gas law wasn't child's play.

Further, note that the "regression" has often been the final part
of a long process of refining experimental technique to even get
good data.  I agree with your obvious next point that GP can help
there too, but not at trivial cost.

> > conceptual innovation
> 
> This is sheer mysticism.  Scientists aren't performing magic,
> they are going through a process, most of which can now be
> specified by algorithms, eg those in GP, Mathematica, 
> statistical analysis packages, etc. 

Nonsense!  Scientists come up with _concepts_ such as black
holes, white holes, wormholes, quarks, atoms, genes, etc,
they're not chugging some simple algorithm... they're doing
very well what people do well.  I agree that it's not magic,
but I'd like to see the Mathematica program for deducing the
*idea* of using zero as a mathematical entity or finding the
*idea* of nuclear fusion.  These are what I mean by conceptual
innovations, and I don't think it's mystical.  It *is* what
scientists do.

> Also it's easiest on us if the
> first tasks are conceptually similar to a nonlinear statistical regression
> task, like the rediscovery of Kepler's laws from orbital data (but
> of course using modern unexplained data).

Agreed, after all we only have toy resources available today.  Where
is unexplained data?  Does anybody know?  I suppose that any high Tc
experiment would qualify, but something simpler is probably better
for our pitiful machines and level of GP understanding.

derek


