From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: No New Continents (was: Future Science)

My take on this:  It seems to me that there are practical limits to
the Science that can be discovered by scientists with particular
capabilities.  The important capabilities are smarts and technology.
Smarts haven't increased much (though they have been in the last
few hundred years because of widely-available books and recently
computational tools).  It seems likely to me that much of the Science
that unaided humans can discover has been gobbled up already.  That
doesn't mean that there aren't many more complex and difficult
scientific discoveries out there for more capable beings to tackle.
As far as I can tell, this has always been the case and I see no
reason to think that it won't continue that way.

Similarly, in Physics, the technological abilities limit the experimental
data requiring explanation.  Tim's SSC example I think rightly shows
up our painful limitations in this regard.  I think it's always been
true that better technology in data collection has always revealed
phenomena requiring new theory, and I can think of no reason why this
trend wouldn't continue.

So I think that for the greater beings we will become, there will be
Science aplenty for inquiring minds and able hands.  There might not
be much sitting around today, though, for unaided humans (discounting
the conceptual innovations that start new fields which are always
possible).  Scientists would do well to look to the edge of capabilities
either in experimental technology or intelligence-assisting technology
(computers, mainly).  This is why I applaud Nick's attempt to push GP
as far as it can go in this direction.

derek


