Date: Thu, 4 Nov 93 7:32:28 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: No New Continents (was: Future Science)

While we're on the "continent" metaphor, and "continents as
basis vectors", it's important to point out that probably less
than 1/100,000,000th of the galaxy's continents have even
been discovered.  There are many solar system bodies that don't
even have "continents" like those on Earth, and many where
typical sphere-based mapping systems go right out the window.
This changes cartography at a very basic level.  And here on
Earth, while we've discovered all the continents, we have yet to map 
most of the ocean floor, and the mapping of the underground, eg oil 
deposits, still leaves much room for improvement.  Now if one
chooses to define "geography" in a very narrow way, as only applying
to Earth's surface, then naturally we eventually exhaust the search 
space.  That doesn't mean we exhaust the search space of geography
defined by its potential rather than traditional scope.
(Extending "geo" to other bodies in the universe, as we do for 
"geology", which also has its theories changed at a very basic 
level when we explore beyond the Earth).

Perhaps that's one of the problems with modern physics -- still
looking for the tradtional things, eg the next big particle,
rather than trying to resolve the many conundrums that have appeared 
in various kinds of data, or searching for new kinds of phenomenon 
to look at.  As I described, there is a large amount 
of unexplained, baffling phenomenon in modern physics compared to the 
turn of the century.  I don't buy the theory that high-Tc
superconductors, solar fusion, etc. are less "basic" than, say, 
the macro phenomenon of traditional thermodynamics, which has
long been an important topic for physics, or that the errors in
these models are necessarily at a "macro" level rather than 
revealing subtle, perhaps quite interesting errors in the
"basis vectors".

As we develop better telescopes and new spacecraft instrumemnts,
a wide variety of new regions and resulting new methods of
mapping them will likely result.  In physics as well, delving into 
new territory with new instruments is likely to unearth new phenomenon 
at a very basic level.  Quantum phenomenon (as measured by STM, NMR, 
quantum dots, etc.) is an area where we are currently opening up quite
a bit of new territory, and astronomy (solar neutrino deficit,
gamma-ray bursters, the search for black holes, cosmic strings, dark 
matter, etc.) is a quite lucrative source of new and/or unexplainable
data.

