Tim May asked for comments.  I afraid this just grew and grew...

> I'm afraid Nick Szabo and I are caught in a conversational attractor
> that others do not seem to be too interested in.

Not any more!  Lack of response does not indicate lack of interest.  
[As I wrote this Ray's comments have popped up.  Argghh!  Beaten
again...]  As they say, I've started so I'll continue. :-)

Shamelessly taking comments out of order and indulging in massive
misrepresentation:

Nick: 
> A mere semantic quibble.   You claimed we've exhausted 80% of the
> physics search space, which amounts to approaching a "final theory"

Tim: 
> Not the whole search space, but about 80% of the "basis vectors," the
> core concepts.

Tim points out that there are far fewer gaps in our knowledge today than
yesterday (astronomy, for instance, which I'll stick with).   I don't
think this is correct for a number of reasons.

Logic.  Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.  The reverse
would be true, namely, that if there were huge gaps in our understanding
of the the universe then we could infer that our fundamental theories
were deficient.  But just because things look nice and rosy now doesn't
mean that we are near a Theory Of Everything and says nothing about how
much there is to go.  

Are there gaps in our picture of the universe?  Yes, plenty.  I'll grant
that our understanding of the universe advanced amazingly earlier this
century.  Einstein (1910s) and Hubble (1920s and 30s) gave us the
expanding universe.  Anymore amazing than recently though?  Stellar
nucleosynthesis wrapped up in the 1950s onwards by Hoyle et al.  The
1970s gave us the beginnings of GUTs and the associated early symmetry
breaking (see Weinberg's "First Three Minutes").  The 1980s gave us
cosmological inflation (and now steady-state-revisted is back on the
table, in the guise of 'eternal' inflation).  Quantum gravity (1960s
onwards), superstrings (another 1970/80s creation depending on how you
view it), and other TOEs (all still speculative) continue to make
predictions which are verified by testing against cosmological models
of the early universe.   No, I think astronomy and cosmology are still
racing full steam ahead, with plenty of loose ends to wrap up: 

-    origin of 3 space dimensions (Klauza-Klein theories say that 
     universe may have a different number of dimensions once) 

-    the creation of the universe (how could we forget that!).  
     No- boundary theorems and quantum gravity look promising here.

-    particle mass spectrum and renormalisation (which impinges on 
     early universe behaviour).  Superstrings, it is hoped, will yield 
     the masses of all particles (different particle types correspond
     to different excitations or waves of the same string loop)

My ancedotal evidence for the pace of change consists of my impression
from browsing Physical Review D.  When I was reading up on the state of
play on wormholes (with Robin Hanson, earlier this year) I was staggered
at the progress since I left college.  I left college in the early 1980s
and hadn't appreciated how much things had moved, in quite fundamental
areas.  Wormholes, quantum mini-blackholes, quasars, cosmic strings,
inflation, baby universes, time travel (and these are just the things
_I'm_ interested in!) - progress is being made in theoretical physics
at an amazing rate across a broad front.  Due, I suspect, largely to
email - almost every author now quotes their email address. 
Collaborative articles get written by scientists who never met, working
on different continents, linked electronically.

Nick
> temporal communication, wormholes, etc. are all areas of active
> interest by top-flight physicists like Kip Thorne)...

Tim:
> [...] These speculative suggestions about time travel, quantum
> teleportation, etc., are not likely to be tested anytime soon, so how
> will GP help?  [...] Using GP to do crossovers of different fields 
> (as an example) may lead to new "theories," but these theories will
> need to be tested and examined.

How can we test the theoretical work on wormholes?  First by numerical
simulation.  I read one article where the formation of a basement
universe was "tested" by running a simulation that modelled the Einstein
equations.  Consider that some theories hold the behaviour of planck
scale wormholes responsible for many of the properties of particles
(that particle may be flux lines trapped in wormholes) - this leads to
a plethora of testable stuff, like the cosmological constant, flatness
problem, particle behaviour.  This is the trend in theory nowadays. 
Particle accelerators and other direct evidence are becoming more
inaccesable, but this is being offset by testing the a theory against
internal or historical conditions.  Is it renormalisable?  Do the
anomalies cancel?  What does it say about the early universe, galactic
formation, perburbation growth.  Do it correctly predict the observed
particle mass spectrum and so on.  This is the arena in which theories
are being tested against each other.  ("Sorry Fred, your theory has five
neutrinos, and the universe would have collapsed 10 billion years ago.") 
So many theories fail at the theoretical stage that some people have
speculated that only one theory is _possible_, subject to imposing some
symmetry conditions that seem to be obeyed.  Or it would when the theory
gets sorted.  Which leads me onto...

The biggest barrier in theoretical physics, IMO, is not the high
energies involved that require things like the supercollider, but the
sheer computational and conceptual problems involved with the subject. 
Superstrings are a good example of this.  I have heard it said that
progress in theoretical physics is drying up (although, as I say, I see
no evidence of this) because of the time it takes for a researcher to
get up to speed on superstrings.  It might take 10 years of single
minded study to make a contribution on superstrings before you burn out
(normally before 30 for theory types), to the exclusion of progress in
other fields.  And most young researchers are sucked into superstrings,
so the story goes, because this looks the most promising area.  

Superstrings are at the stage that general relativity was at between
1907-1915.  The wooly concepts are there, but we just don't have a full
set of mathematical tools to knock everything into shape.  Einstein was
lucky that for general relativity, which rests on the concept of the
equivalence princple, tensor calculus already existed.  With
superstrings, so I'm told by knowledgable chaps, we're not so lucky. 
If GP could produce such tools - Nobel prize at the very least!

>> Let's debate them.  Why 19th and early 20th?  Why not 18th or 
>> 10th or 3rd BC?  Why not mid and late 20th?  Why not 21st century? 

> Many reasons why the late 19th, early 20th century was so successful:

      [good reasons elided]

What about the 16 and 17th C?  They were successful as well.  We had
Descartes, Galileo, Kepler, Newton, Leibniz, Halley, Wallis.. => optics,
calculus, planetary cosmology, mechanics, gravity.  Early 19th C
Faraday, Maxwell and Heavyside => electromagnetism.

One major nit-pick:
> - synergism between compelling (and essentially complete) theories
> of statistical mechanics, electromagnetism, and quantum mechanics....
> all of these were formulated in the 1880-1900 period in essentially
> their modern form (QM needed another 30 years to be adequately
> completed).

Must disagree on quantum mechanics.  There's a _lot_ more to QM than
black-body radiation, or even the photoelectric effect (1905).  The
"new" quantum theory (post 1925/6 due to Heisenberg and Schrodinger)
differed completely from the "old" (1900 - 1924, due to Planck, Einstein
and Bohr).  The new quantum theory then turned into quantum field
theory, ending up with QED in the 1940s due to Schwinger and Feynman. 
QED is often described as the most successful theory ever and is the
progenitor for all the modern unified field theories.  All that could
be said is that QM _started_ in 1900.  If you're going to declare that
EM was 'done' in 1880-1900 then you would have to say that QM was done
in 1924-7.  I would rather say that EM was done from 1820 - 1880 and
give QM from 1900 - 1930.

No, science looks like a rather steady climb.  New theories are
continuing to turn the world upside down, much as they have for the past
4 centuries since the renaissance.  I don't know how long this will go
on for, but neither does anyone else.  At college we were told that
theoretical physics was coming to an end - 20 years and it would all be
over.  That was 10 years ago.  I don't think anyone now believes even
the next 20 years will sew everything up.  With superstrings people
mutter more like the "middle of the next century, with luck".  Surprises
all the way down, is my prediction.  Our understanding of the universe
is slowly creeping back to zero-time, but like a log-scale zero might
be infinitely far away.

> Later work, on quarks for example, did not (and very likely
> will not, in the next few hundred years) produce the same cornucopia
> of resulting derivavite theories

All GUTs are based on quarks.  Quark states have testable implications
in the early universe and, it now seems according to Bethe's latest
work, in neutron stars, which may have quarkonic cores and layers.

> and technologies. Science fiction writers may be enamored of
> "gravitonic" and perhaps even "quarktronic" devices, but nearly
> everything you see around you comes from the basic work in
> thermodynamics, electromagnetic theory, and quantum mechanics.

The technological implications of any of this are a separate subject,
as both Nick and Tim agreed earlier.  I think it's too early to pass
judgement on quarks and femtotechnology yet.

To use Tim's continents metaphor, I would say that some continents have
been mapped, but others have a heart of darkness, with their edges
poking out of the mists.  In uncharted regions dragons and sea serpents
lurk.  We don't even know that the world is round (although we suspect
it is), let alone it's circumference!

