rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu says:
>   Could someone summarize many of the great unexplained fundamental
> problems in physics today? Tim's looking for dragons, there's probably
> a few. Here's some off the top of my head:

I think Tim has missed a fundamental problem in physics.

Geography as Tim has noted was almost fully developed in terms of an
understanding of the basic shapes of all the world's landmasses by the
18th century -- but an understanding of the forces that created those
landmasses waited until the 19th century, and even the early 20th.

Biology is now nearly explained in terms of chemistry. Chemistry is
now mostly explained in terms of physics.

However, physics, in spite of its apparent triumph, still has a blind
spot the size of Mount Rushmore.

Before modern chemistry arose, people had no real idea how chemistry
functioned. They produced accurate measurements of the properties of
substances, recorded with extreme accuracy how substances transformed
into others via reactions, etc, but there was no fundamental
understanding of what lay beneath. Now there is an understanding, a
deep understanding, in terms of more fundamental processes of physics.

Biology at one time was also in this boat -- people could describe
cells qualitatively and quantitatively, but could not explain what was
really going on at a deep mechanical and chemical level -- and now
they can.

Physics has elegant equations that describe things well, just as the
premodern chemists could tell you precisely how much cinnibar would
yield how much gas when you heated it. There is a lack of a
fundamental understanding, however. We poke and we prod at the
components of the universe -- we know how matter moves when we do
things to it, we explain how something cools when we heat it, etc, but
we still have no real knowledge of WHY.

Just as the psychiatrists still stumble around acting like alchemists
with their cargo-cult methodologies in terms of vague descriptions,
just as the socialists stumble about with vague descriptions of
processes they pretend to understand but only dimly percieve (in spite
of the power of classical economics and its descendants), our
physicists still haven't gone far enough in one crucial respect --
they have no understanding of the deep structure of the things they
are describing.

Dreams of a final theory occured before in physics. Many of us forget
that newtonian mechanics explains the universe very well for purposes
of measurement. Except for a few anomalies, it was largely believed in
the 19th century that physics was now understood. Then came
electromagnetism, radioactivity, and a huge can of worms was opened
that caused the whole of this confidence in the depth of our knowledge
of physics to unravel.

Physics has been abandoning pretentions to be able to really explain
things for a while -- it only pretends to be able to predict, and its
predictions are good. However, so were the predictions of many of the
better alchemists -- they knew their materials, and could tell you
exactly what they would do under a wide variety of circumstances. The
more "patchwork" has appeared in physics (the disunity of gravitation
from the other forces, the differing behavior of matter at the large
and small scale, etc) the less confidence I have had that what we are
approaching is truly a slow slide into a final theory that will look
much like what we have had up to now. I have the gut reaction that
what we are sliding towards is a spot where suddenly some great genius
rips open the curtain and reveals a hidden structure that we have
completely lost sight of the search for. For too long we have taken
the movement of the mechanical dolls on the stage to have been the
reality and ignored the possibility of a clockwork within them.

