compare it to creating _outlines_. Knowledge is chunked into
categories, into major branches and sub-branches. (There is no
guarantee that these branches are primary, that the outline is
optimal. For example, we call physics and biology major fields, and
biophysics a derived field. But a different culture might've
identified physics and biophysics (called something else, natch) as
primary, and biology would then be derivative of other fields.)

In most areas, such as physics, geography, geology, and so on, the
large structures, the outline headings, get discovered at some rate.
The identification of the large structure of the field "saturates" at
some point, that is, the main features have been identified.

I have used the term "knowledgequakes" in my k-space model for those
relatively rare circumstances where a brand new continent is
discovered, where old fields are suddenly seen to be related in some
unexpected way. An example would be Darwin's basic idea of evolution.

Evolution sent seismic waves throughout nearly all branches of
knowledge, causing basis vectors to realign and alter. Every field had
to at least act itself "Are we affected by this new idea?" Some
fields, like economics, were of course dramatically affected.

Ipso facto, there is no reason to expect fields of knowledge to be
infinitely deep, with new discoveries of a major sort always around
the bend. I view this in algorithmic information theory terms, but
there may be other ways of viewing this.

Thus, in my view, the universe appears "comprehensible" to finite
agents like ourselves. The oft-mentioned "unreasonable effectiveness
of mathematics" is key.

Once again, as in our AIT VirtSem, I recommend the book "Complexity,
Entropy, and the Physics of Information," edited by W. Zurek.

--Tim May


