Nick Szabo and Derek Zahn are mentioning Mathematica, the symbolic and
general math program from Wolfram Research, so as a Mathematica user,
let me comment:

> > And GP is far from the only tool available.  The math known to the 
> > Egyptians is child's play for Mathematica.  Heck, most of the math 
> > known before the 20th century is child's play for Mathematica.
> 
> Well, no kidding!  I think it would be vastly unfair, for example, to
> take some GP with a full suite of analysis tools, "rediscover" the
> laws of gravity, and claim success.  That's simply hindsight.  I
.....
> very well what people do well.  I agree that it's not magic,
> but I'd like to see the Mathematica program for deducing the
> *idea* of using zero as a mathematical entity or finding the
> *idea* of nuclear fusion.  These are what I mean by conceptual
> innovations, and I don't think it's mystical.  It *is* what
> scientists do.

Mathematica (henceforth MMA) is quite stupid. One of the biggest
issues in using MMA and similar math programs (Maple, Macsyma) is
being proficient enough in math to know when and how it can be used.
MMA is especially stupid about making blunders in finding roots,
polynomial factors, etc.

This is not due to poor programming, necessarily, but due to some
ambiguities in mathematics itself. 

Granted, MMA-like programs will get much better with time, and,
granted, Gauss would've had a ball with a program like MMA, but I
agree more with Derek's point that the _concepts_ of math (like the
concept of zero, of integration, of factoring itself) are not "known"
to MMA, and hence it can only do mechanistic operations under the
direction of someone who _does_ know the concepts and can use them to
uncover new knowledge.

This whole topic of automated discovery of new science is intriguing.
Newell and Simon thought they had it in the 1950s with the General
Theorem Prover, Lenat thought he had it with Eurisko, Derek just
mentioned Bacon, and so forth. (I can recall in 1966 seeing the output
of a program which "discovered" new proofs in classical geometry,
including a novel proof about Isoceles triangles.)

Now the Great Hope is some mix of neural nets, genetic programming,
and the like. Maybe. But I'll hold my enthusiasm until I actually
_see_ some new knowledge of a nontrivial sort being discovered.

Forget the "back-trading" that "discovered" Kepler's Laws--let's see
some _new_ laws discovered!

--Tim May

